---
/** /design, the chips: every vocabulary rendered by the component that ships it. */
import CollectionChips from "../CollectionChips.astro";
import HueFilter from "../HueFilter.astro";
import KindChip from "../KindChip.astro";
import StatusChip from "../StatusChip.astro";
import TagChips from "../TagChips.astro";
import VerdictBadge from "../VerdictBadge.astro";
import { STATUSES } from "../../lib/experiments";
import { KINDS, library, libraryTags } from "../../lib/library";
import { monogram } from "../../lib/post";
import { collectionGroups, siteGroups } from "../../lib/sites";
import { MONOGRAM_HUES, hueSlot } from "../../lib/tags";
import { VERDICTS } from "../../lib/tools";

/** Three real collections, for the chip in its filter-link form. */
const collections = collectionGroups.slice(0, 3).map((group) => group.slug);

/** Five real tags. Tags are plain grey, so any five show the whole chip. */
const tagSpecimen = libraryTags.slice(0, 5).map((group) => group.slug);

/**
 * The identity palette's seven slots, as the monogram wears them, off real
 * posters (`styles/chip.css › [data-hue]`). A slot no saved post lands on has
 * no specimen, which is the honest thing for a page that renders shipped data.
 */
const posts = library.flatMap((entry) => (entry.post === null ? [] : [entry.post]));

const monogramSpecimen = Array.from({ length: MONOGRAM_HUES }, (_, hue) =>
  posts.find((post) => hueSlot(post.handle) === hue),
).flatMap((post) =>
  post === undefined
    ? []
    : [{ hue: hueSlot(post.handle), initial: monogram(post.author), author: post.author }],
);
---

<section>
  <h2 class="sheet__head mono">Chips</h2>
  <p>
    A soft tint, never a solid fill, so twenty rows read as one page rather
    than as a traffic light. A tone sets three numbers and the tint, the
    hairline and the ink all derive from them, which is how light and dark
    stay in step without a second palette.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every ink clears 6:1 over its own tint. Not over the page behind it: over
    the colour the tint actually composites to, which is the lower of the two
    numbers and the one you are subject to. Measuring against the page
    flatters every tone by about eight tenths of a point.
  </p>

  <dl class="spec">
    <div class="spec__row">
      <dt class="code">verdict</dt>
      <dd>
        <ul class="chips">
          {VERDICTS.map((verdict) => (
            <li>
              <VerdictBadge verdict={verdict} />
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </dd>
    </div>
    <div class="spec__row">
      <dt class="code">status</dt>
      <dd>
        <ul class="chips">
          {STATUSES.map((status) => (
            <li>
              <StatusChip status={status} />
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </dd>
    </div>
    <div class="spec__row">
      <dt class="code">kind</dt>
      <dd>
        <ul class="chips">
          {KINDS.map((kind) => (
            <li>
              <KindChip kind={kind} />
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </dd>
    </div>
    <div class="spec__row">
      <dt class="code">chip--link</dt>
      <dd><CollectionChips collections={collections} /></dd>
    </div>
    <div class="spec__row">
      <dt class="code">tag</dt>
      <dd><TagChips tags={tagSpecimen} /></dd>
    </div>
    <div class="spec__row">
      <dt class="code">hue</dt>
      <dd><HueFilter groups={siteGroups} base="/sites" /></dd>
    </div>
    <div class="spec__row">
      <dt class="code">monogram</dt>
      <dd>
        <ul class="chips chips--monogram">
          {monogramSpecimen.map((one) => (
            <li>
              <span class="monogram" data-hue={one.hue} title={one.author}>
                {one.initial}
              </span>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </dd>
    </div>
  </dl>

  <p>
    The tag is the one chip that is not a tint. A verdict and a status sit
    somewhere on a scale, and the fill is what says so; a library tag names
    a subject, and one subject is not better than another. So it takes no
    colour at all: a grey outlined box with the word in it.
  </p>

  <p>
    The hue row is <a href="/sites">/sites</a> by colour: the tag&rsquo;s box
    with a dot where the word goes, and the count of sites after it. The
    families come off each capture&rsquo;s stored palette at build time,
    greys and creams left out, so a site with no colour left is black and
    white. A dot is the one colour here held to no contrast bar, because a
    yellow dark enough for 3:1 on white is mustard; its hairline edge does
    that job. Press one to open the gallery filtered.
  </p>

  <p>
    The monogram row is the site&rsquo;s seven identity hues. A saved post on <a
      href="/library/kind/post">/library</a> opens with the poster&rsquo;s
    profile picture, or without one, a circle holding their initial,
    coloured by hashing their handle into one of seven slots.
  </p>

  <p>
    Those are every verdict on <a href="/tools">/tools</a>, every status on <a
    href="/experiments">/experiments</a> and every kind on <a
    href="/library">/library</a>, read from the same lists those pages
    use.
  </p>
  <p>
    The chip--link row is the filter form: same tone, same size, plus a
    pointer cursor and a tint that deepens on hover. Hover one. That state
    holds AA rather than 6:1, the one place the vocabulary spends contrast
    on purpose.
  </p>
  <p>
    Blue is the accent hue held back from the accent lightness, because an
    inline link and a shipped chip must not be the same blue on the same
    page. It is held back by two dials in the light theme and by lightness
    alone in the dark one, where nothing darker survives the tint.
  </p>
</section>

<style>

  /* --- chips ------------------------------------------------------------ */

  /* The same row `CollectionChips.astro › .collections` uses, at the same gap,
     because the chip in its label form and the chip in its link form are one
     vocabulary and a row of them should not be two spacings. A list, so four
     verdicts are four items to anything reading the page out loud. */
  .chips {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.4rem;
    margin: 0;
  }


  /* Seven 48px circles need more air between them than a row of chips does,
     and they need it in both directions once the row wraps. */
  .chips--monogram {
    gap: 0.6rem;
  }
</style>
